.\" Man page for the dak configuration file. See man(7) and groff_man(7).
.TH DAK-CONFIG 5 "2026-09-28" "dak 1.0.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
dak\-config \- configuration file format for
.BR dak (1)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dak
is driven entirely by a JSON configuration file, conventionally named
.IR config.json .
The file describes one or more devices and a set of scenes; a scene assigns
content to the button screens and binds actions to button presses, encoder
turns and timers.
.PP
JSON has no comment syntax, but
.B dak
strips comments before parsing, so both
.B //
line comments and
.B /* ... */
block comments may appear anywhere the JSON grammar allows whitespace.
Comment markers inside a string are part of the string value.
.SH "TOP\-LEVEL STRUCTURE"
The top level is a JSON object with up to six keys, all optional:
.TP
.B version
The config schema version the file was written for, a string of the form
.I MAJOR.MINOR
(digits only, no leading zeros, e.g.
.BR "\(dq1.0\(dq" ).
When absent the file is taken to be of the supported version.
This version of dak supports config version 1.0.
.IP
The schema version is not the version of
.BR dak :
.I MAJOR
is raised only when a change breaks existing configs (a key renamed or
removed, or its meaning changed),
.I MINOR
when a change only adds something.
A file with the same major version and the same or a lower minor version
loads normally.
A higher minor version loads with a warning, since it may use settings this
.B dak
does not know yet (those are then reported as errors as usual).
A different major version is refused on its own, without the other errors it
would cause: a higher one needs a newer
.BR dak ,
a lower one has to be updated as described in the release notes.
A malformed version is a configuration error as well.
The version is printed on startup.
.TP
.B variables
Declared, typed values that actions and setup parameters read and assign. See
.B VARIABLES
below.
.TP
.B defaults
Press\-detection timing knobs, connect\-time brightness levels, the button
background and text colours, the text markup syntax and the fonts. See
.B DEFAULTS
below.
.TP
.B scenes
The scenes dictionary, including the special
.B on_start
scene. See
.B SCENES
below.
.TP
.B devices
The individual device definitions, keyed by logical device id. The
.B \-\-map
wizard of
.BR dak (1)
prints one definition in exactly this shape. See
.B DEVICES
below.
.TP
.B logging
Where log lines go and how detailed they are. See
.B LOGGING
below.
.SH DEFAULTS
The optional
.B defaults
object tunes press detection, the brightness applied when a device
connects, the colours used to draw button images and text, how button text is
marked up and which fonts draw it. Every key is optional and falls back to its
built\-in value.
.nf
.RS 4
"defaults": {
  "short_press_duration": 300,
  "double_click_gap": 300,
  "button_brightness": 50,
  "encoder_brightness": 50,
  "background": "#000000",
  "text_color": "#ffffff",
  "device_reconnect_interval": 15,
  "device_reconnect_max_attempts": 0,
  "markup": "tmux",
  "fonts": { "regular": "~/.fonts/Iosevka\-Regular.ttf" }
}
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B short_press_duration
Milliseconds (default
.BR 300 ).
A press released at most this long after it started is a
.BR short_press ;
a press held longer is a
.BR long_press .
.TP
.B double_click_gap
Milliseconds (default
.BR 300 ).
Two presses form a
.B double_click
when the second lands within this long of the previous release.
.TP
.B button_brightness
Percent
.B 0
to
.B 100
(default
.BR 50 ),
applied to every device's button/screen LCDs when it connects.
.TP
.B encoder_brightness
Percent
.B 0
to
.B 100
(default
.BR 50 ),
applied to every device's encoder LED ring when it connects. Some units have
no functioning encoder LEDs; the value is sent regardless.
.TP
.B background
A colour (default
.BR #000000 )
composited under every image that has an alpha channel, and the canvas text is
drawn on. Without it, an icon's transparent pixels would keep whatever colour
is hidden under them (usually white), because the device's image format has no
alpha. See
.B COLOURS
below.
.TP
.B text_color
A colour (default
.BR #ffffff )
that button text is drawn in. See
.B COLOURS
below.
.TP
.B device_reconnect_interval
Whole seconds, at least
.B 1
(default
.BR 15 ),
between attempts to get a device back after it disappeared; the first attempt
is made right away. See
.B DEVICES
below.
.TP
.B device_reconnect_max_attempts
How many reconnect attempts to make before giving up on a device (default
.BR 0 ,
meaning unlimited).
.TP
.B markup
How button text is parsed:
.B tmux
(default) understands the
.B #[...]
tags described under
.BR "TEXT MARKUP" ;
.B none
shows text exactly as written. A setup entry's own
.B markup
key overrides it.
.TP
.B fonts
An object naming font files to draw button text with, instead of the embedded
fonts; see
.BR FONTS .
.PP
Both reconnect keys may also appear in a single device definition, where they
override the
.B defaults
value for that device only.
.SH COLOURS
A colour value is either a
.B #RRGGBB
hex literal (six digits, case\-insensitive) or one of the named colours
.BR black ,
.BR silver ,
.BR gray " / " grey ,
.BR white ,
.BR maroon ,
.BR red ,
.BR purple ,
.BR fuchsia " / " magenta ,
.BR green ,
.BR lime ,
.BR olive ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR navy ,
.BR blue ,
.BR teal ,
.BR aqua " / " cyan
and
.BR orange .
The
.B #RGB
shorthand and alpha values are not accepted. A bad colour in the
.B defaults
section or in a literal setup
.B background
or
.B text_color
is a hard config error.
.PP
.B background
and
.B text_color
are also writable at runtime with a
.B $defaults
assignment (see
.BR "Assigning to a variable" ),
and a per\-button setup entry can override either with its own
.B background
or
.B text_color
key (see
.BR setup ).
A per\-button override belongs to the button, not the scene: like the rest of a
button's content it is kept until a later scene explicitly redefines that
button. Changing a colour at runtime affects the next draw (a scene switch, a
refresh tick or a new image); buttons already drawn are not repainted.
.SH "TEXT MARKUP"
With
.B markup
set to
.B tmux
(the default), the text of a
.BR text ,
.B text_value
or
.B text_exec
entry may contain tmux status\-line style tags. A tag
.B #[attr,attr ...]
changes how the text after it is drawn; attributes are separated by commas
and/or spaces:
.TP
.BR bold ", " nobold
Bold on or off.
.TP
.BR italics ", " italic ", " noitalics ", " noitalic
Italic on or off.
.TP
.B none
Bold and italic off.
.TP
.B default
Everything back to the defaults: plain, the button's colours, centred.
.TP
.BI fg= colour
Text colour (see
.BR COLOURS ),
or
.B fg=default
for the button's
.BR text_color .
.TP
.BI bg= colour
Highlight painted behind the characters, or
.B bg=default
for none. The button's own background is still
.BR background .
.TP
.BR align=left ", " align=centre ", " align=center ", " align=right
Placement of the line. Alignment applies to a whole line; the last
.B align
in effect at the end of a line decides it.
.TP
.BI u= hex
A
.B dak
extension (not tmux): inserts the Unicode character with that code point, 1 to
6 hex digits. Bare hex tokens right after it insert more characters, so
.B #[u=1F44D,1F3FD]
equals
.BR #[u=1F44D,u=1F3FD] .
.PP
.B ##
is a literal
.BR # ;
any other
.B #
not starting a tag is shown as\-is. Styles and alignment carry over to the
following lines until changed. A tag that does not parse (unknown attribute,
bad colour or code point, no closing
.B ]
on the same line) is shown literally and logged as a warning; in a literal
.B text_value
it is also reported when the config loads. For example:
.nf
.RS 4
"params": "#[align=left,bold]CPU\en#[fg=red]$cpu%"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Markup is interpreted after
.B $
references are expanded, so
.B #[fg=$alert]
works. In a
.B text_exec
command that runs through
.B sh \-c
(one containing
.BR | ,
.BR ; ,
.B >
and similar), an unquoted
.B #
starts a shell comment: write
.B "echo '#[bold]hi' | cat"
rather than
.BR "echo #[bold]hi | cat" .
.PP
A line shows at most 6 columns: ordinary characters take one, wide
characters such as emoji and CJK ideographs take two, and a character is never
split. There is no text shaping: variation selectors and zero\-width joiners
are dropped, and an emoji made of several code points (skin tone, joined
sequence, flag) shows only its first one.
.PP
Tags are applied even where their text is not shown: lines are cut to 6
columns and the button to 3 lines only after the whole text is parsed, so a
style set in the hidden part still carries over to later lines, and the last
.B align
of a line decides its alignment even past the cut.
.PP
Right\-to\-left scripts are not supported: characters are drawn left to right in
stored order, so Hebrew appears mirrored and Arabic letters unjoined. Print the
text already reordered and reshaped (for example with
.B python\-bidi
and
.BR arabic\-reshaper )
as a workaround.
.SH FONTS
Button text is drawn with fonts embedded in
.BR dak :
DejaVu Sans Mono in regular, bold, oblique and bold oblique, which all share one
character width, plus the monochrome Noto Emoji for characters DejaVu lacks.
.B defaults.fonts
may name other TrueType/OpenType files, each optional:
.nf
.RS 4
"fonts": {
  "regular": "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/iosevka/Iosevka\-Regular.ttf",
  "bold": "...", "italic": "...", "bold_italic": "...",
  "emoji": "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/noto/NotoColorEmoji.ttf",
  "extra": "/usr/share/fonts/opentype/noto/NotoSansCJK\-Regular.ttc#0"
}
.RE
.fi
.TP
.BR regular ", " bold ", " italic ", " bold_italic
The font for text in that style.
.TP
.B emoji
The fallback for characters none of the text fonts have (emoji, symbols).
.TP
.B extra
A last\-resort font tried after the emoji fonts, typically for
Chinese/Japanese/Korean.
.PP
Paths expand a leading
.B ~
and
.B $
references (with the variables' initial values). A path ending in
.BI # N
picks face
.I N
(from 0) of a
.B .ttc
font collection. Files are loaded once at startup; a missing, unreadable,
larger than 256 MiB or invalid font file (including one whose line height,
.B M
width or units per em make no sense) is a config error, and fonts cannot be
changed at runtime.
.PP
Each character is looked up in the configured font of its style, then the
embedded font of that style, then (for bold/italic text) the configured
.B regular
font, then the configured
.B emoji
font, then the embedded Noto Emoji, then the configured
.B extra
font; if none has it, a box is drawn. A character both an emoji font and
.B extra
have is thus drawn as an emoji. Characters from the emoji fonts and
.B extra
are fitted into their columns. A
proportional font works, but lines of wide letters are then drawn smaller to
fit.
.PP
The embedded fonts have no CJK characters (ideographs, kana, Hangul, half\-width
and full\-width forms); name a CJK font as
.B extra
(Latin stays in DejaVu; CJK in the font's one weight, so
.B #[bold]
does not embolden it unless
.B bold
also names a CJK Bold font) or as
.B regular
(all text in that font). In
.B NotoSansCJK\-Regular.ttc
the face selects the region:
.B #0
Japanese,
.B #1
Korean,
.B #2
Simplified Chinese,
.B #3
Traditional Chinese,
.B #4
Hong Kong.
.PP
A glyph is drawable when the font has an outline or a colour bitmap (CBDT or
sbix, PNG or BGRA). Bitmap emoji are drawn as pictures in their own colours;
.B fg=
does not affect them. COLR and SVG colour glyphs cannot be drawn. Every
configured font is scanned at startup: a font with no drawable character (a
COLR\- or SVG\-only colour font) is a config error; a COLR/SVG font that also
has outlines is used in monochrome with a warning; and characters a font
cannot draw are counted in one warning and fall back to the slot's embedded
font. With
.B "\-d fonts"
every font is listed in lookup order, and each configured font's undrawable
characters are shown as code point ranges grouped by reason (COLR colour
layers, SVG colour picture, unsupported bitmap, probably unsupported sbix
image, empty glyph), at most 20 lines per font.

.SH VARIABLES
The optional
.B variables
object declares named values that actions and scene setup parameters read and
assign. Variables are global: one set shared by every device and every scene,
initialized once at startup. Every variable must be declared; referencing an
undeclared one is a config\-load error.
.nf
.RS 4
"variables": {
  "count": { "type": "int", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 10 },
  "label": { "type": "str", "max_length": 8, "value": "hello" },
  "plain": { "type": "int" }
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
In each entry the key is the variable name, which must start with a letter and
contain only letters, digits and underscores. The names
.BR var ,
.BR defaults ,
.BR version ,
.B scenes
and
.B devices
are reserved and cannot be declared.
.TP
.B type
Required. Either
.B int
(a 32\-bit signed integer) or
.B str
(a variable\-length unicode string).
.TP
.B min ", " max
Integer variables only, optional and inclusive. They default to the full
32\-bit range and bound an assigned value.
.TP
.B max_length
String variables only, optional. Defaults to
.B 255
and may be at most
.BR 65535 ;
an assigned string is truncated to it.
.TP
.B value
Optional. The value the variable starts with, defaulting to
.B 0
or the empty string. A value outside the declared range or of the wrong type
is a hard config error.
.PP
Unknown keys and type\-inappropriate parameters (for example
.B min
on a
.BR str )
are hard config errors.
.SS "Reading a variable"
A
.B $
starts a reference.
.B $name
reads a declared variable;
.B $var.name
is the same thing written with its explicit scope (the
.B var
scope is the default). The form
.B $defaults.<name>
reads a
.B defaults
parameter
.RB ( button_brightness ,
.BR encoder_brightness ,
.BR short_press_duration ,
.BR double_click_gap ,
.BR device_reconnect_interval ,
.BR device_reconnect_max_attempts ,
.BR background ,
.BR text_color ,
.BR markup ;
the last three are strings, the rest int), reflecting any runtime changes. A
colour reads back exactly as it was set, so a name stays a name.
.PP
References are substituted in every action string, in setup
.B params
and in the
.B timer
seconds key, and are resolved when the value is actually used, so they always
see the current value. An int renders as its decimal digits; a str is inserted
verbatim, with no quotes added.
.PP
A name is greedy:
.B $name_kun
reads
.BR name_kun .
Escape the next character with a backslash to end it:
.B $name\e-kun
is the value of
.B name
followed by
.BR \-kun .
A backslash before a dollar sign,
.BR \e$ ,
is a literal dollar sign, and
.BR \e\e
is a literal backslash; any other backslash is left alone for the command
tokenizer. A
.B $
not followed by a variable name is a config error.
.PP
Because a dollar sign always starts a reference, a literal dollar sign in a
command must be written
.BR \e$ .
This is a change from configs written before variables existed, where a shell
.B $VAR
was passed through unchanged.
.SS "Assigning to a variable"
An action value of the form
.B "$<target> <op> <rhs>"
assigns:
.TP
.B <target>
A declared variable (or
.BR var.name ),
or one of the writable
.B defaults
parameters: the brightness keys
.B button_brightness
and
.BR encoder_brightness ,
and the colour keys
.B background
and
.BR text_color .
The read\-only timing, reconnect,
.B markup
and
.B fonts
parameters cannot be assigned.
.TP
.B <op>
.B :=
clamps or truncates and warns,
.B ~=
does the same silently, and
.B =
rejects an out\-of\-range, over\-long or otherwise invalid value.
.TP
.B <rhs>
An integer literal, a
.B "quoted string"
(which is a string even where a number is expected), another variable
.RB ( $b ),
or a
.B $(command)
substitution.
.PP
An int is clamped into its
.B min
to
.B max
range, a str is truncated to its
.BR max_length ,
and a wrong\-type right\-hand side is always a config error. With
.B ":= / ~=,"
the target's declared default is used when the value cannot be applied, with a
warning for
.BR := .
.PP
A colour target takes a string right\-hand side that parses as a colour (see
.BR COLOURS ).
There is nothing to clamp, so a value that is not a colour is discarded in
favour of the configured default instead: a bad literal is a config error under
.BR = ,
a warning under
.B :=
and silent under
.BR ~= .
A colour that comes from a variable or a command can only be checked when the
action fires, and is always handled leniently -
.B =
behaves like
.B :=
there - so an invalid value warns and resets to the configured default rather
than failing at runtime.
Assignments write runtime state only;
.I config.json
is never modified.
.SS "Command substitution"
.B $(command)
runs when the action fires and converts the output to the target's type: an
int target parses the first non\-empty line (trimmed) and clamps it; a str
target takes the whole output with trailing newlines stripped and truncates it;
a colour target parses the first non\-empty line (trimmed) as a colour.
For
.BR = ,
an unconvertible output, a non\-zero exit or a timeout is an error and the
variable is left unchanged; for
.B :=
and
.BR ~= ,
the target's default is used instead. A colour is the exception: because its
output is only known at runtime there is nothing to clamp, so
.B =
behaves like
.B :=
and an invalid colour warns and uses the configured default.
.SH SCENES
The
.B scenes
object maps scene names to scene definitions. A scene name can be any text;
the special name
.B on_start
is reserved and runs when the program starts.
.PP
Each scene has two reserved keys:
.B setup
(what the buttons show) and
.B actions
(what the controls do). A missing key means "empty". Button content from the
previous scene is kept for any button the new scene does not mention in
.BR setup .
.SS "Control references"
Buttons and encoders are addressed by references of the form
.IR <device><b|e><number> :
.IP \(bu 2
.I <device>
is a single digit
.B 1
to
.B 9
naming a logical device, matching a key under
.BR devices .
.IP \(bu 2
.B b
addresses a button, and
.B e
addresses an encoder.
.IP \(bu 2
.I <number>
is the control number, always written with two digits,
.B 01
to
.BR 99 .
.PP
For example,
.B 1b01
is button 1 on device 1 and
.B 2e01
is encoder 1 on device 2.
.PP
References to devices not present are skipped at runtime with a warning.
Encoder references work in
.B actions
but not in
.BR setup :
a setup entry on an
.B e
key is a config error. Assigning an image to a button without a display is
skipped at runtime with a warning, and out\-of\-range control numbers are
skipped with a warning.
.SS "setup"
The
.B setup
object maps a control reference to an object with a
.B type
and
.BR params ,
plus an optional
.BR refresh ,
.BR background ,
.B text_color
and
.BR markup .
.TP
.B image
.IB params " is a path"
to an image file to load onto the button. It must be a regular file (a device
or FIFO is refused), read within 5 seconds. The formats read are PNG, JPEG, GIF,
BMP, ICO, WebP and PNM (the netpbm formats). An image may be at most 4096
pixels wide and high; larger ones are refused as an error, and big ones are
scaled down before they are drawn.
.TP
.B image_exec
.I params
is a command run asynchronously whose stdout must be a valid image file, in
one of the formats and within the size limit of
.BR image .
If it
does not finish within 5 seconds, or the button is changed in the meantime,
the process is killed, an error is logged, and the button shows the text
.B Error
in red.
.TP
.B text
.I params
is a path to a file; the first 6 columns of its first 3 lines are shown, with
markup applied (see
.BR "TEXT MARKUP" ).
.TP
.B text_value
.I params
is text shown directly, after
.B $
references are expanded, without reading a file or running a command. For
example, a button can show a variable's value with
.BR "$defaults.button_brightness%" .
.TP
.B text_exec
.I params
is a command run asynchronously; its stdout is shown the same way as
.BR text .
A timeout or a reassignment kills it and draws
.B Error
in red, as with
.BR image_exec .
.TP
.B launch
.I params
is a command run fully detached: its own process group, no standard
input/output, re\-parented to init when
.B dak
exits. Nothing is drawn on the button and nothing is restored on termination.
.TP
.B clear
Clear the button image.
.TP
.B background
A colour override (default: the
.B defaults.background
value). Allowed on
.BR image ,
.BR image_exec ,
.BR text ,
.B text_value
and
.BR text_exec .
For an image it is composited under transparent pixels; for text it is the
canvas the glyphs are drawn on.
.TP
.B text_color
A colour override for the glyphs (default: the
.B defaults.text_color
value). Allowed on
.BR text ,
.B text_value
and
.BR text_exec
only.
.TP
.B markup
.B tmux
or
.B none
(default: the
.B defaults.markup
value), how this entry's text is parsed. Allowed on
.BR text ,
.B text_value
and
.BR text_exec
only, and not subject to
.B $
expansion.
.TP
.B refresh
Seconds, default
.BR 0 .
Allowed on
.BR image ,
.BR text ,
.BR text_value ,
.B image_exec
and
.BR text_exec ;
re\-applies that entry every
.B refresh
seconds without touching other buttons. A value of
.B 0
or an omitted key means the entry is applied once on scene entry. A refresh
restarts an
.B image_exec
or
.B text_exec
the same way a reassignment does, killing any still\-running process for that
button. It is not allowed on
.B clear
or
.BR launch .
.SS "actions"
The
.B actions
object maps a control reference to that control's bindings. A button binds the
events
.BR pressed ,
.BR released ,
.BR short_press ,
.B long_press
and
.BR double_click ;
an encoder additionally binds
.B turn_cw
and
.BR turn_ccw ,
one action per rotation notch, and its knob push uses the same five press
events.
.PP
.B pressed
and
.B released
fire on the down and up edges. The complex events are decided on the release
edge: a press held longer than
.B short_press_duration
fires
.BR long_press ;
a second press landing within
.B double_click_gap
of the previous release fires
.BR double_click
(and its first click never fires
.BR short_press );
anything else is a
.BR short_press ,
which fires once the gap has passed with no second press.
.SS "The timer"
The special key
.B timer
maps to a single\-entry object whose key is a number of seconds and whose
value is an action:
.nf
.RS 4
"timer": { "5": "@Main" }
.RE
.fi
The action runs once that many seconds after the scene is entered. The
seconds must be at least 1; a variable that holds less is treated as 1.
.SS "Limits"
Keypad input and program output come from outside dak, so its work is
bounded:
.IP \(bu 2
At most 50 input events (presses, or encoder notches) per control per second
are acted on; the rest are dropped with a warning, a dropped press together
with its release.
.IP \(bu 2
At most 32 action commands and
.B $(command)
assignments run at the same time, across all keypads; an action that would
start another is skipped with a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR image_exec ,
.B text_exec
and
.B $(command)
programs run in their own process group, which is killed as a whole when the
program times out, fails or is no longer needed, so the programs it started do
not outlive it. A program that succeeded may leave background jobs running.
.IP \(bu 2
A keypad that disconnects more than 5 times within a minute is reconnected
only after a pause, from 1 second doubling up to 1 minute.
.SS "Action values"
An action value is one of:
.IP \(bu 2
.B @
(stay on the current scene and re\-apply its setup)
.IP \(bu 2
.B @scene
(jump to the named scene)
.IP \(bu 2
.B "$<target> <op> <rhs>"
(assign; see
.B VARIABLES
above)
.IP \(bu 2
anything else, taken as a command path followed by its arguments
.IP \(bu 2
an array of any of the above, whose entries run without waiting on each other
.PP
A
.B $
reference anywhere in a value is substituted before the value is used.
.PP
A value may also be an array, for example
.B "[\(dq/usr/bin/notify-send hi\(dq, \(dq@Main\(dq],"
to trigger more than one action from one event. An empty array is the array
form of "bound but no action"; an empty string inside a non\-empty array is
rejected. At most one scene\-changing entry
.RB ( @
or
.BR @scene )
is allowed per array.
.SS "Commands and the shell"
A command runs directly when it contains no unquoted shell operator, and
through
.B sh \-c
when it contains one of
.B "| & ; < > ` ( )"
or a newline. Pipelines and redirection therefore work when written, while a
plain command never grows a shell. Only the config text decides this, never the
value of a variable.
.SS "Values in commands"
A variable may hold any text, including shell syntax, and that text often
comes from other programs. Inside a command, a
.B $name
value is therefore always data:
.IP \(bu 2
In a plain command it is exactly one argument, or part of one, as written: it
is not split at spaces, its quotes and backslashes are kept, and a leading
.B ~
in it is not expanded. An empty value is an empty argument.
.IP \(bu 2
In a command that runs through
.BR "sh \-c" ,
each value is passed to the shell as a separate argument and the script only
expands it, as
.BR \(dq${1}\(dq ,
.BR \(dq${2}\(dq ,
and so on, quoted for where the reference appears. The shell never parses the
value itself. The script therefore sees these values as its positional
parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
A value never changes what kind of action runs:
.B @$name
switches to the scene the value names, and a command stays a command whatever
its values contain.
.PP
.B $!name
(and
.BR $!scope.name )
pastes the value in as if it were config text: it is split into words, may add
shell syntax, and as a whole action may be
.B @scene
or an assignment. This is how
.B $name
behaved before version 0.15.0. Use it only for values you control, never for
text another program produced.
.PP
A leading
.B ~
in a command line, in
.BR image_exec / text_exec
params, or in a
.B launch
value, is expanded to the home directory:
.B ~
alone becomes
.BR $HOME ,
and
.B ~/rest
becomes
.BR $HOME/rest .
This applies to every word of the line. A
.B ~
anywhere but the start of a word is left untouched, and there is no
.B ~user
support.
.SS "Parallel execution"
Actions in an array are not ordered against each other: each command and each
.B $(command)
assignment runs on its own task. Do not rely on the order of independent
assignments within one array.
.SH DEVICES
The
.B devices
object maps a logical device id (a single digit
.B 1
to
.BR 9 )
to a device definition. Each definition is the exact JSON that
.B "dak \-\-map"
prints:
.nf
.RS 4
{
  "device_id": "0300:3002",
  "device_name": "Ajazz HOTSPOTEKUSB HID DEMO",
  "serial": "unknown",
  "key_count": 9,
  "encoder_count": 3,
  "screens": 6,
  "protocol_version": 2,
  "buttons": [
    { "number": 1, "press": 1, "release": 1, "screen": true, "draw_id": 1 }
  ],
  "encoders": [
    { "number": 1, "cw": 144, "ccw": 145, "press": 146, "release": 146 }
  ]
}
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B device_id
The device's USB vendor and product id as
.BR "vvvv:pppp" .
.TP
.B device_name
The name reported over USB, printed when the device connects.
.TP
.B serial
The device serial number, or
.B unknown
for devices without one. A definition whose serial is not
.B unknown
matches only the device reporting that exact serial, which tells identical
devices apart. A definition whose serial is
.B unknown
falls back to comparing
.BR device_id .
When more than one attached device matches a definition, none of them is used
and a warning is printed: serial numbers are not secret, so a second device
reporting the same one may be impersonating the keypad. A lost keypad is only
reconnected to a device with the same serial it had.
.TP
.B key_count ", " encoder_count
The number of buttons and encoders the device has, at most 254 each.
.TP
.B screens
The number of button LCDs.
.TP
.B protocol_version
Optional. Selects which connection protocol variant to use, overriding the
value recognized from the device id: 1, 2 or 3 (a device without any serial
number is switched to the oldest protocol automatically). Omit it or set it to
JSON
.B null
to use the default.
.TP
.BR device_reconnect_interval ", " device_reconnect_max_attempts
Optional. Override the
.B defaults
values of the same name for this device.
.TP
.B buttons
One entry per button, mapping its
.B number
to the raw codes reported for press and release, whether it has a
.B screen
and its
.BR draw_id .
.TP
.B encoders
One entry per encoder, mapping its
.B number
to its clockwise
.RB ( cw )
and counter\-clockwise
.RB ( ccw )
turn codes and, after the wizard replays a knob push, its
.B press
and
.B release
codes. An encoder without push codes still turns, but its knob push is
ignored.
.PP
At startup every definition is matched against the discovered hardware, and
every matched device is connected and driven with the shared scenes. A device
defined in config but not found is reported with a warning, and a discovered
device with no definition is ignored with a warning. When no configured
device is found, the program exits with an error.
.PP
A device that disappears while dak runs (host suspend, unplugging, a USB bus
reset) does not stop the program. dak warns
\(lqdevice #\fIN\fR disconnected ...; waiting for it to come back\(rq,
and looks for the same device, matched as at startup: right away, then every
.B device_reconnect_interval
seconds, for up to
.B device_reconnect_max_attempts
attempts (0 means forever). When they run out it reports
\(lqdevice #\fIN\fR did not come back after \fIM\fR attempts; giving up on it\(rq
and stops driving that device, releasing its lock; the others keep running.
Sending
.B SIGUSR1
starts a fresh round of attempts. Once no device is left, dak exits with
status 4 when run from a terminal, and keeps waiting for
.B SIGUSR1
when running as a service (see
.BR dak (1)).
When the device is back dak prints
\(lqdevice #\fIN\fR reconnected ...\(rq. It then reapplies the current brightness and repaints what
the buttons showed before: the current scene, buttons inherited from earlier
scenes and all variables are kept, refreshing buttons resume, and
.B text_exec
and
.B image_exec
programs run again. A key held at the moment of the disconnect counts as
released. Ctrl\-C or
.B SIGTERM
still stops dak while waiting. Only a failure of a device's
first connection at startup is fatal, and only for that device.
.PP
On FreeBSD reconnecting may be flaky and needs more troubleshooting: after an
unplug through VM USB passthrough the keypad was once not re\-enumerated by
the kernel, and dak kept waiting for it.
.SH LOGGING
The optional
.B logging
object says where the program's log lines go and which of them are written.
Without it every line goes where
.B auto
picks, at level
.BR info .
All keys are optional:
.TP
.B output
One output name or an array of them (each at most once):
.RS
.TP
.B auto
The default. The systemd journal when stderr is connected to it (systemd sets
.BR JOURNAL_STREAM ),
syslog once
.BR dak (1)
has detached from its terminal, the console otherwise. Cannot be combined with
other outputs.
.TP
.B console
Informational and debug lines on stdout, warnings and errors on stderr.
.TP
.B journal
Every line on stderr, prefixed with its syslog priority in angle brackets
(\(lq<4>warning: ...\(rq), which journald records as the entry's priority, so
.B journalctl \-p warning
works.
.TP
.B syslog
Sent to
.BR syslog (3)
as
.B dak
with the process id, using
.BR syslog_facility .
.TP
.B file
Appended to
.BR file .
.RE
.TP
.B file
The log file for the
.B file
output; a leading
.B ~
is expanded. Defaults to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/dak/dak.log ,
else
.IR ~/.local/state/dak/dak.log .
Missing directories are created (mode 0700) and the file is created with mode
0600. A symlink, a file with more than one hard link, a file owned by another
user or anything that is not a regular file is refused. A file that cannot be
opened stops the program at startup. It is
reopened on
.BR SIGHUP ,
so it can be rotated. Setting it without the
.B file
output only produces a warning.
.TP
.B syslog_facility
The facility syslog lines are sent with:
.BR user " (the default), " daemon ", " local0 ", " local1 ", " local2 ", " local3 ,
.BR local4 ", " local5 ", " local6 " or " local7 .
.TP
.B level
The most detailed level written:
.BR error ", " warning ", " info " (the default) or " debug .
Errors are always written;
.B warning
hides the banner and the connect and disconnect lines.
.TP
.B debug
An array of debug subsystems to enable, as with
.BR "dak \-d" :
.BR device ", " scene ", " actions " (or " action ") and " fonts .
Their lines are only written when
.B level
is
.BR debug .
.TP
.B timestamps
.BR auto " (the default), " true " or " false
(as JSON booleans or strings).
.B auto
stamps only log file lines, since the journal and syslog stamp every entry
themselves;
.B true
stamps console, journal and file lines;
.B false
none.
.PP
The command line takes precedence:
.B \-\-log\-level
replaces
.BR level ;
.B \-d
adds subsystems and, without
.BR \-\-log\-level ,
raises the level to
.BR debug ;
.B \-\-log\-file
and
.B \-\-syslog
add their outputs next to the configured ones. Lines written before the
configuration is read (the banner, the config location and configuration
errors) always go to the console, or the journal under systemd.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS 4
{
  "scenes": {
    "on_start": {
      "setup": {
        "1b01": { "type": "image", "params": "/path/reader.ico" }
      },
      "actions": {
        "1b01": { "short_press": "@Main" },
        "timer": { "1": "@Main" }
      }
    },
    "Main": {
      "setup": {},
      "actions": {}
    }
  },
  "devices": {
    "1": {
      "device_id": "0300:3002",
      "device_name": "Ajazz HOTSPOTEKUSB HID DEMO",
      "serial": "unknown",
      "key_count": 9,
      "encoder_count": 3,
      "screens": 6,
      "protocol_version": 2,
      "buttons": [
        { "number": 1, "press": 1, "release": 1, "screen": true, "draw_id": 1 }
      ],
      "encoders": []
    }
  }
}
.RE
.fi
